<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch-formulae-and-variations"
         xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">

  <title>Formulae and Variations</title>

  <introduction>
  <p>Mathematics helps us describe relationships between quantities. A <term>formula</term> provides a rule that connects variables, while <term>variation</term> helps us understand how one quantity changes when another changes.</p>

    <p>For example:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>What happens to the area of a rectangle when its length is doubled?</li>
      <li>How does travel time change when speed increases?</li>
      <li>How many workers are needed to finish a job in less time?</li>
    </ul>

    <p>In this chapter, you will learn how to:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Change the subject of a formula to find an unknown quantity.</li>
      <li>Model relationships using direct, inverse, joint, and partial variation.</li>
      <li>Apply these ideas to real-life situations and mathematical modelling.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Understanding formulae and variation allows us to analyse patterns, make predictions, and solve practical problems.</p>
  </introduction>

